    whyChooseContent: "Most restoration companies are mitigation specialists that subcontract the reconstruction phase to other contractors. This creates handoff delays, quality inconsistencies, and divided accountability. Strata Property Services is different — as a comprehensive building services company, we have the in-house trades to manage the entire restoration process from emergency response through complete reconstruction. One company, one project manager, one standard of quality from start to finish.",
    industryInsight: "The building restoration industry is evolving rapidly in response to increasing water damage frequency, rising insurance costs, and growing awareness of indoor air quality issues. Water damage claims in Strata buildings have increased dramatically over the past decade, driven by aging infrastructure, more extreme weather events, and higher property values that increase restoration costs. Insurance deductibles have risen correspondingly — many Strata corporations now face $25,000-$100,000+ deductibles per water damage occurrence. This has made prevention and rapid response more critical than ever, and has increased the value of restoration contractors who can minimize damage scope through prompt professional mitigation.",
    materialsAndMethods: "Our restoration work uses professional-grade materials throughout. Structural drying employs LGR (low grain refrigerant) dehumidifiers, high-velocity air movers, and injectidry systems for wall cavity drying. Antimicrobial treatments use EPA-registered products appropriate for the contamination category. Reconstruction materials match or exceed the original specifications — Type X drywall for fire-rated assemblies, premium joint compounds, quality flooring materials, and professional-grade paints and coatings. All materials are selected for their performance, durability, and compatibility with the restoration environment.",
    strataSpecificContent: "Strata building restoration involves navigating complex relationships between the Strata corporation, individual unit owners, property managers, insurance companies, and sometimes municipal authorities. We act as the central coordinator, ensuring all stakeholders are informed, documentation is shared appropriately, and the restoration proceeds efficiently. For multi-unit events (a burst riser affecting multiple floors, for example), we develop comprehensive restoration plans that address all affected areas while maintaining habitable conditions in unaffected portions of the building. Our project managers are experienced in Strata environments and understand the communication, scheduling, and compliance requirements these projects demand.",
    seasonalConsiderations: "Restoration work is inherently reactive — emergencies don't follow a schedule. However, seasonal patterns do affect the types of restoration events we respond to. Winter brings freeze-related pipe bursts and ice dam water intrusion. Spring brings snowmelt-related flooding and the discovery of winter damage. Summer brings reduced insurance activity but is an ideal time for preventive maintenance and building assessment. Fall is the season for preparing buildings against winter risks — insulating exposed pipes, servicing heating systems, and clearing drainage pathways. We recommend annual building assessments in fall to identify and address vulnerabilities before winter weather creates emergency conditions.",
    safetyAndCompliance: "All restoration work follows IICRC S500 (water damage), S520 (mold remediation), and S540 (fire damage) standards. Our crews are trained in personal protective equipment requirements for each contamination category. Asbestos testing is performed before demolition in pre-1990 buildings. Lead paint testing is conducted before disturbing painted surfaces in pre-1978 buildings. WorkSafeBC safety protocols are followed for all construction activities. We maintain comprehensive insurance coverage including pollution liability appropriate for restoration work."
